{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>First occurrence</b><br>I have gathered. Like \"and I will bestow\" (ארוה), like \"all who pass by the way.\"" "My forest. One says it is like \"you emptied out the honey,\" and one says it is a reed, from the Targum, placed at the end [in the forest]. And the meaning is a sweet reed." "<b>Second occurrence</b><br>And behold, he departed, and she longed for him after he said, \"I have come to my garden,\" and she said: \"I gathered my myrrh, and I fed my friends and companions, and gave them to drink until they were sated.\"" "<b>Third occurrence</b><br>The Shekhinah said: I have come to my resting place, to my place above, after I received the wine libation and the bread of the Presence and the first fruits, and Israel rejoiced and ate peace offerings. And the meaning is \"To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices to Me, says YHWH; I am sated with burnt offerings\" — as it says, \"For every beast of the forest is Mine,\" and if I needed, I have everything."], :book :song-of-songs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {1 "The bracketed [ביערה] and parenthetical (מ) in 'קנה (מ)תרגו' appear corrupt or abbreviated in the source; translated hyper-literally. 'Sweet reed' (קנה סוקר/סוכר) = sugarcane.", 3 "Bracketed clause [כענין כי לי כל חיתו יער ואלו הוצרכתי יש לי הכל] is a parenthetical scriptural analogy with gloss; rendered literally. 'אלים' in 'עולות אלים' rendered as 'Elohim' per voice rules, though contextually it may read as 'to Me.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra's commentary on the Canticles" "http://beta.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002030344/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:44:27.384537304Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}